PHASE THREE: DETAILED EXPLANATIONS

SECTION A: INDIAN RAILWAYS AND INFRASTRUCTURE (Q1–Q25)

Q1. B — 2,633 route kilometres.

Kavach Version 4.0 had been commissioned on 2,633 route kilometres by 31 July 2026. This included 1,423 route kilometres on the Delhi–Mumbai route and 1,210 on the Delhi–Howrah route.

Q2. B — 1,423 and 1,210.

The first figure refers to Delhi–Mumbai, and the second to Delhi–Howrah. The trap is reversing the figures or confusing the combined total with either individual route.

Q3. B — Automatic train protection.

Kavach is designed to help prevent collisions and unsafe train movements through automatic protection functions. It is not a ticketing platform or a station reservation system.

Q4. B — Route kilometre.

RKM measures the length of a railway route. It differs from track kilometre, which can count multiple parallel tracks separately. A double-track route can therefore have more track kilometres than route kilometres.

Q5. D — 21,794 route kilometres.

This was the reported extent of trackside Kavach implementation work, which is distinct from the 2,633 route kilometres on which Version 4.0 had been commissioned. Work underway and commissioned coverage are not interchangeable statistics.

Q6. C — ₹2.93 lakh crore.

The railway budget grant for FY 2026–27 was reported as ₹2,93,030 crore. Do not confuse the total grant with expenditure recorded during a particular month or quarter.

Q7. B — Vande Bharat is a train; Kavach is a safety system.

Vande Bharat refers to a train platform and its services, while Kavach is a railway protection technology. Their purposes differ even though both are associated with railway modernisation.

Q8. C — Indian Railways.

Indian Railways is the national railway system. NHAI manages national highways, DFCCIL implements dedicated freight-corridor projects, and the Airports Authority of India has responsibilities in civil aviation infrastructure.

Q9. A — Railway station redevelopment.

The Amrit Bharat Station Scheme aims to improve station infrastructure, passenger facilities, accessibility and the overall station environment.

Q10. B — 1,340 identified; 261 completed.

These were the figures reported as of 18 July 2026. The important distinction is between the total number of selected stations and the subset whose redevelopment had been completed by the reporting date.

Q11. A — Kavach.

Automatic train protection can intervene when specified unsafe conditions arise. UTS is associated with unreserved ticketing, IRCTC with railway-related services and FOIS with freight operations information.

Q12. B — Dedicated freight infrastructure.

Dedicated Freight Corridors provide railway capacity specifically for freight. Separating freight traffic from passenger services can improve capacity, operational efficiency and transit reliability.

Q13. B — Electrification supplies traction infrastructure; Kavach protects train movements.

Electrification enables electric traction through an appropriate power-supply system. Kavach addresses train safety. One is not a substitute for the other.

Q14. A — Gati Shakti Vishwavidyalaya.

This university focuses on transportation and logistics-related education, research and professional development, including railway-sector needs.

Q15. A — High-speed passenger rail.

The Mumbai–Ahmedabad project is a high-speed rail corridor designed to provide faster intercity passenger travel. It should not be confused with a freight corridor.

Q16. D — 350 km/h.

The reported design speed for the Mumbai–Ahmedabad High-Speed Rail Corridor was 350 km/h. Design speed describes the intended engineering capability; it does not mean every service operates at that speed.

Q17. A — Integrated multimodal infrastructure planning.

PM Gati Shakti is intended to coordinate infrastructure planning across transport modes and other sectors. Its purpose includes reducing planning gaps and improving connectivity between projects.

Q18. A — Amrit Bharat Station Scheme.

The scheme targets station redevelopment. PM Gati Shakti focuses on integrated infrastructure planning, whereas the Amrit Bharat Station Scheme focuses on railway stations.

Q19. C — Approximately 99.6%.

The July 2026 factsheet reported railway electrification at about 99.6%. The published figure should not be rounded into a claim of universal completion.

Q20. B — Electricity generated through a hydrogen fuel cell.

A fuel cell converts chemical energy into electrical energy through an electrochemical reaction. The train uses that electricity for propulsion rather than relying necessarily on diesel combustion.

Q21. A — India's first indigenously developed hydrogen fuel-cell trainset was reported launched in July 2026.

This is a time-sensitive 2026 development, not a longstanding railway fact. The cited infrastructure factsheet identifies the Jind–Sonipat section.

Q22. B — 10 coaches.

The reported trainset had ten coaches and an approximate capacity of 2,600 passengers. Such numbers should be revised with their reporting date because train specifications and deployment plans can change.

Q23. A — Jind–Sonipat.

This is the railway section identified in the 2026 infrastructure factsheet for the hydrogen fuel-cell trainset. It should not be confused with the Mumbai–Ahmedabad high-speed rail corridor.

Q24. A — Communication and data transmission.

Optical fibre carries information as light signals. In railway applications, it supports telecommunications, signalling-related data transmission and other operational communications.

Q25. B — Amrit Bharat Station Scheme: station redevelopment.

Kavach is a protection system, Vande Bharat is a train platform, and a Dedicated Freight Corridor is designed for freight movement. The other options deliberately mix unrelated railway functions.

SECTION B: NATIONAL PROGRAMMES, ECONOMY AND GOVERNANCE (Q26–Q40)

Q26. A — Ministry of Finance.

The Ministry of Finance handles the Union government's budget process, including expenditure proposals, receipts and fiscal planning.

Q27. B — Allocation is not the same as expenditure.

A budget allocation provides for spending; actual expenditure records what has been spent. A ministry may spend less than its allocation. The figures should be compared for the same period and accounting basis.

Q28. B — Reserve Bank of India.

RBI is India's central bank. Its functions include monetary policy, currency management and regulation or supervision of important parts of the financial system.

Q29. A — SEBI.

The Securities and Exchange Board of India regulates the securities market and aims to protect investors and support orderly market functioning. RBI and SEBI have different regulatory responsibilities.

Q30. A — Digital India.

The initiative promotes digital infrastructure, online public services and digital empowerment. It is not an environmental conservation or maritime infrastructure programme.

Q31. A — Farmers.

PM-KISAN provides income support to eligible farmer families under the scheme's rules. Eligibility conditions matter; it should not be described as a universal payment to every person working in agriculture.

Q32. A — Finance Commission.

The Finance Commission recommends how certain tax revenues should be shared between the Union and states, along with other fiscal matters specified in its terms of reference.

Q33. A — Election Commission of India.

The Election Commission conducts elections to Parliament, state legislatures, and the offices of President and Vice-President under the constitutional framework. Local-body elections are handled by state election commissions.

Q34. A — Ayushman Bharat.

Ayushman Bharat encompasses initiatives aimed at improving healthcare access. Its PM-JAY component provides health coverage to eligible beneficiaries under the applicable scheme provisions.

Q35. A — UPI is a digital payment system.

The Unified Payments Interface facilitates interoperable digital payments. It is unrelated to railway protection, satellite navigation or environmental measurement.

Q36. A — Destination-based indirect tax framework.

GST is generally structured around the destination or place of consumption of goods and services. It is distinct from direct taxes such as personal income tax.

Q37. A — Comptroller and Auditor General of India.

The CAG is a constitutional authority responsible for auditing government accounts and expenditure under the applicable legal framework.

Q38. A — Distribution of essential food commodities.

The Public Distribution System supports access to food grains for eligible households. It is a food-security mechanism, not a financial-market or transport-regulation programme.

Q39. A — A sustained rise in the general price level.

Inflation refers to an overall increase in prices. It does not require every product's price to rise equally, and its effects on real wages depend on how incomes change relative to prices.

Q40. A — NITI Aayog.

NITI Aayog replaced the Planning Commission in 2015. It serves as a policy think tank and supports cooperative federalism and strategic policy development.

SECTION C: SCIENCE, TECHNOLOGY AND SPACE (Q41–Q55)

Q41. A — ISRO.

The Indian Space Research Organisation develops and operates major elements of India's civilian space programme, including launch vehicles, satellites and planetary missions.

Q42. B — DRDO.

The Defence Research and Development Organisation develops technologies and systems for India's defence needs. ISRO focuses on space technology, although the two organisations' work can have overlapping technological applications.

Q43. A — The lunar south polar region.

Chandrayaan-3 landed in the Moon's southern polar region in August 2023. The mission demonstrated India's lunar soft-landing capability and deployed the Vikram lander and Pragyan rover.

Q44. A — The Sun.

Aditya-L1 is India's solar observatory mission. The L1 region provides a useful vantage point for continuous solar observations with limited interruption from Earth.

Q45. A — Positioning, navigation and timing.

Satellite navigation systems provide information that receivers use to determine location and time. India's NavIC is its regional satellite navigation system.

Q46. A — A launch vehicle carries payloads; a satellite is a space object or spacecraft.

A launch vehicle transports a payload into space. A satellite may then operate in orbit for communications, navigation, observation or scientific research.

Q47. A — Electrochemical conversion.

A hydrogen fuel cell converts chemical energy into electricity through electrochemical reactions. Unlike a combustion engine, it does not rely on burning fuel to drive a mechanical cycle.

Q48. A — Solar energy.

Solar energy is renewable because sunlight is continually replenished on human timescales. Coal, petroleum and natural gas are fossil fuels formed over geological timescales.

Q49. A — Artificial Intelligence.

AI refers to computational systems capable of tasks such as pattern recognition, prediction, language processing and decision support. It is a broad field, not a single machine or algorithm.

Q50. A — Cellular wireless versus optical fibre.

5G delivers mobile communications over radio links, while fibre-optic systems transmit data through light in physical cables. They are complementary: fibre often provides the backhaul supporting mobile networks.

Q51. A — CERT-In.

The Indian Computer Emergency Response Team coordinates responses to specified cybersecurity incidents, issues advisories and supports cyber-incident management.

Q52. A — Remote observation of Earth and its environment.

Remote sensing satellites collect data without requiring direct physical contact with the observed surface. Applications include agriculture, land-use mapping, disaster monitoring and resource assessment.

Q53. A — Carbon dioxide.

Carbon dioxide absorbs and emits infrared radiation and contributes to the greenhouse effect. Argon, helium and neon are not significant direct greenhouse gases under ordinary atmospheric conditions.

Q54. A — Splitting a heavy atomic nucleus.

Nuclear fission releases energy when a heavy nucleus splits into smaller nuclei. Nuclear power stations use controlled fission reactions to produce heat, which is converted into electricity.

Q55. B — Watt.

The watt is the SI unit of power. A joule measures energy, a coulomb measures electric charge, and an ohm measures electrical resistance.

SECTION D: DEFENCE, SECURITY AND INTERNATIONAL AFFAIRS (Q56–Q65)

Q56. A — Ministry of Defence.

The ministry oversees defence policy and administration at the Union government level. The armed services carry out their operational roles within the national defence framework.

Q57. A — Armed Forces.

The Indian Army, Navy and Air Force are India's three principal military services. They specialise in land, maritime and air operations, respectively.

Q58. A — Bilateral means two parties; multilateral means more than two.

These terms describe participation in diplomacy, agreements and exercises. The number of participating parties is the defining distinction, not the size of the exercise.

Q59. A — NATO.

The North Atlantic Treaty Organization was founded in 1949 as a collective-defence alliance. It is different from global organisations concerned with public health, trade and education.

Q60. A — New York City.

The United Nations headquarters is in New York. The UN also has major offices in Geneva, Vienna and Nairobi, which explains why these cities are common distractors.

Q61. A — World Health Organization.

WHO coordinates international public-health work and provides technical guidance. It is not the body that sets international trade rules.

Q62. A — World Trade Organization.

WTO administers agreements and provides a framework for trade negotiations and dispute settlement among members. The ILO deals with labour, and WHO deals with health.

Q63. A — International economic and financial cooperation.

The G20 is a forum for major advanced and emerging economies to discuss global economic issues, financial stability and related policy concerns.

Q64. A — International Civil Aviation Organization.

ICAO develops international standards and recommended practices for civil aviation. It is not the organisation responsible for maritime shipping standards.

Q65. A — International Maritime Organization.

IMO develops international rules and standards for shipping safety, security and environmental performance. Its scope is maritime transport rather than global monetary policy.

SECTION E: ENVIRONMENT, ENERGY AND CLIMATE (Q66–Q75)

Q66. A — Paris Agreement.

Adopted in 2015, the agreement seeks to strengthen the global response to climate change, including efforts to limit the rise in global average temperature.

Q67. A — Methane.

Methane is the principal constituent of most conventional natural gas. It is also a potent greenhouse gas, so leaks during extraction and transport are an important climate concern.

Q68. A — Project Tiger.

India's Project Tiger was launched in 1973 to support tiger conservation through protected areas, habitat management and related measures. It should not be confused with Project Elephant.

Q69. A — Solar energy cooperation.

The International Solar Alliance promotes cooperation to expand solar-energy deployment, particularly through international collaboration and mobilisation of investment.

Q70. A — Coal.

Coal is non-renewable because its formation takes geological timescales. Wind, sunlight and tidal flows are naturally replenished energy sources.

Q71. A — Fuel cells generate electricity electrochemically.

In a hydrogen fuel cell, hydrogen reacts electrochemically with oxygen, generating electricity and typically producing water and heat. A hydrogen fuel-cell vehicle is not necessarily battery-only, although it may include a battery for energy management.

Q72. A — Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change.

The IPCC assesses scientific literature on climate change, impacts, adaptation and mitigation. It synthesises research rather than functioning as a direct operational weather-forecasting agency.

Q73. A — Variety of life.

Biodiversity includes variation within species, differences between species and the diversity of ecosystems. It is a biological concept, not a measure of energy output or transport infrastructure.

Q74. A — National Green Hydrogen Mission.

The mission aims to support the development of India's green-hydrogen ecosystem, including production, use, technology and associated infrastructure.

Q75. A — Solar photovoltaic cells convert light into electricity.

Photovoltaic cells produce electrical energy from incident light. Wind turbines convert wind energy into mechanical rotation and then electricity; hydroelectric plants use flowing or falling water.

SECTION F: AWARDS, SPORTS, IMPORTANT DAYS AND ORGANISATIONS (Q76–Q85)

Q76. A — Physics.

Physics is one of the Nobel Prize categories, alongside chemistry, physiology or medicine, literature and peace. The Sveriges Riksbank Prize in Economic Sciences in Memory of Alfred Nobel is also commonly discussed alongside the Nobel prizes, but it was established separately.

Q77. A — International Cricket Council.

The ICC is the international governing body for cricket and organises or oversees major international competitions under its authority.

Q78. A — Association football.

FIFA is the international governing body for association football, commonly called soccer in some countries. It should not be confused with the ICC, which governs cricket.

Q79. A — International Olympic Committee.

The IOC oversees the Olympic Movement and the Olympic Games framework. International sports federations govern the rules of their individual sports.

Q80. A — 8 March.

International Women's Day is observed annually on 8 March. Remember the date rather than relying on the day of the week, which changes each year.

Q81. A — 5 June.

World Environment Day is observed on 5 June. It is associated with environmental awareness and action, including pollution, biodiversity and ecosystem issues.

Q82. A — 21 June.

International Yoga Day is observed annually on 21 June. The date coincides with the June solstice period in the Northern Hemisphere.

Q83. A — 10 December.

Human Rights Day marks the adoption of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights on 10 December 1948.

Q84. A — International Monetary Fund.

The IMF publishes the World Economic Outlook, which assesses global economic conditions, growth projections and economic risks.

Q85. A — United Nations Development Programme.

UNDP publishes the Human Development Report, which examines development through a broader lens than income alone, including health, education and living standards.

SECTION G: INDIAN POLITY, ENGINEERING AND HIGH-VALUE CONFUSING FACTS (Q86–Q100)

Q86. A — Part III.

Fundamental Rights are contained in Part III of the Indian Constitution, covering Articles 12–35. Part IV contains the Directive Principles of State Policy.

Q87. C — Article 51A.

Article 51A sets out the Fundamental Duties of citizens. Article 21 concerns protection of life and personal liberty, while Article 32 concerns constitutional remedies.

Q88. C — Article 32.

Article 32 gives individuals the right to approach the Supreme Court for enforcement of Fundamental Rights. Article 14 concerns equality before the law and equal protection of the laws.

Q89. B — Five years.

The normal term of the Lok Sabha is five years from the date appointed for its first meeting, unless it is dissolved earlier. The Rajya Sabha, by contrast, is a continuing House and is not dissolved.

Q90. B — Introduction only in the Lok Sabha.

A Money Bill can be introduced only in the Lok Sabha, subject to the Constitution's requirements. The Rajya Sabha has a limited role in relation to a Money Bill and must return it within 14 days with recommendations.

Q91. A — Supreme Court of India.

The Supreme Court is India's highest judicial body. It exercises constitutional, appellate and other jurisdiction under the Constitution and relevant laws.

Q92. A — Newton.

Force is measured in newtons. One newton is the force required to accelerate a one-kilogram mass at one metre per second squared.

Q93. B — Pascal.

Pressure is force per unit area and its SI unit is the pascal. One pascal equals one newton per square metre. The joule measures energy, the ampere measures current, and the tesla measures magnetic flux density.

Q94. A — Ammeter.

An ammeter measures electric current and is connected in series in a conventional circuit arrangement. It is designed to have very low resistance.

Q95. B — Voltmeter.

A voltmeter measures potential difference and is connected in parallel across the component being measured. It is designed to have high resistance so that it draws minimal current.

Q96. A — Power is the rate of energy transfer.

Energy is measured in joules, while power is measured in watts. One watt equals one joule per second, making the distinction important in electricity and mechanical engineering questions.

Q97. A — Ohm.

The ohm is the SI unit of electrical resistance. Ohm's law expresses the relationship between voltage, current and resistance under the relevant conditions: voltage equals current multiplied by resistance.

Q98. A — Silicon.

Silicon is widely used in semiconductor devices because its electrical conductivity can be controlled through doping and device design. Copper, aluminium and silver are commonly used as conductors.

Q99. C — 1,676 mm.

Indian broad gauge is 1,676 mm. The 1,435 mm gauge is known as standard gauge, while 1,000 mm is metre gauge. Gauge questions are especially important for railway examinations.

Q100. A — Regulating train movements safely.

Railway signals communicate movement authorities or operating instructions to train crews and help maintain safe separation between trains. Signalling works together with interlocking, operating rules and, where fitted, train-protection systems.

